  "discovery_metadata": {
    "doi": "10.1088/1402-4896/ae73bc",
    "title": "Porous carbon materials from peanut shells for high-rate and long-cycle supercapacitors",
    "score": 12,
    "date": "2026-05-27",
    "abstract": "Abstract Biomass-derived porous carbons are promising candidates for supercapacitor electrodes due to their high surface area, tunable pore structures, and superior conductivity. In this study, peanut shells were utilized as a sustainable precursor to synthesize hierarchical porous carbon via H 3 PO 4 activation. The resulting materials feature a well-defined micro-mesoporous architecture, achieving a specific capacitance of 221.9 F g −1 at 0.5 A g −1 in a three-electrode configuration. Furthermore, the symmetric supercapacitor demonstrated exceptional long-term durability, with 99.3% capacitance retention over 20 000 cycles at 10 A g −1 . These findings offer a scalable and efficient strategy for converting agricultural waste into high-performance energy storage materials.",
